    "title": "Understanding the statistical geography of Summer Hill",
    "section": "",
    "text": "The Australian Statistical Geography Standard (ASGS)\nIsn’t that a truly boring place to start? Bear with me. The ASGS is just another why to define the boundaries of where we live, like postcode or Local Government Areas. The advantages of the AGSM:\n\nIt is hierarchical—larger areas areas are defined by grouping smaller areas. (unlike postcodes and LGAs for example)\nThe ABS provides a lot of data in this structure.\n\nWe will consider two levels in this geography standard:\n\nStatistical Area Level 2 (SA2)\nStatistical Area Level 1 (SA1)\n\n\nStatistical Area Level 2 (SA2)\nFigure 1 shows map of the Summer Hill district overlayed with the eight intersecting SA2s. Note that the SA2s represent reasonably large and connected local communities.\n\n\nTap or hover on the map to see see the SA2 name\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFigure 1: The Summer Hill district covers eight SA2s\n\n\n\n\nThe most populous SA2 is Marrickville - South (n = 14,287). Only a tiny sliver of Croydon Park - Enfield (n = 556) lies within the Summer Hill district (see Table 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTable 1: The Summer Hill district covers eight SA2s\n\n\n\n\n\n\nStatistical Area Level 1 (SA1)\nFigure 2 shows map of the Summer Hill district overlayed with the intersecting SA1s. Note that the SA1s represent much more local areas—just a few streets or blocks.\n\n\nTap or hover on the map to see see the SA2 name and SA1 code\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFigure 2: The Summer Hill district covers eight SA2s\n\n\n\n\nIn total there are 202 SA1s across the Summer Hill district. These range in population size from 50 to almost 900 people; the average population size is about 400.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTable 2: The Summer Hill district covers 202 SA1s\n\n\n\n\nNow that we understand the geography, we an use ABS census data to characterise the Summer Hill district in terms of its demographics.",
